From: WallView
Subject: [PATCH] WebPageProxy: coalesce touch moves while the page has not
 answered the previous touch event

Outside iOS every touch event is sent to the web process as it arrives.
The NSPanel reports 120 touch moves per second, and a page whose
touchmove handler takes longer than that interval builds up a queue on
the main thread: on a Home Assistant view with a chart (about 100 ms per
touchmove) the answer to a touch event came up to 2.1 s after the event
was sent, so the page handled touch moves, painted tiles and applied
state updates seconds after the finger had left, and every stale move
cost the same main thread time as a current one.

Keep at most one touch move back while an earlier touch event is
unanswered, replace it with a newer one that has the same touch points
in the same states, and send it when the answer arrives. Any other touch
event sends the held move first, so the order of events and the last
position before a touch end are unchanged. The page gets one touchmove
per turn of its event loop with the newest position, which is what other
engines deliver to a busy page. Gesture recognition in the UI process is
not affected: it sees every event before this queue.

WALLVIEW_WEBKIT_NO_TOUCH_COALESCING (any value) sends every touch move
as before, for A/B measurements.

Upstream-Status: Pending
